  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe cold front is now through the area and pushing off to the\neast into eastern PA. Some clearing across the area is underway\nbut, the northwest flow has begun in earnest and lake induced\nstratocumulus is now streaming through the area. By this \nevening, some rain and snow showers will develop.\n\nChances of lake-enhanced showers increase north of I-80 after \nsunset which may transition to lake-enhanced snow showers \novernight. Any accumulations on pavement/asphalt will be \nchallenged given recent warm temperatures, but some light \naccumulations on grassy/elevated surfaces are possible. High- \nend possibilities for accumulations only total ~1\" for northern \nMercer/Venango County, but most RRFS members show a light \naccumulation favored, primarily north of I-80. Evening if this\naccumulation does occur, there will mainly be amounts in the\ngrass. \n\nMostly clear skies are favored Tuesday night, which will\ncertainty bring the coolest temperatures of the week, and\npossibly some of the coldest temperatures of this season thus \nfar. Widespread 20s are forecast for lows, with some isolated \nteens possible in small, insulated drainage basins. In some\ncases, this is 5 to 7 degrees below normal for the low\ntemperature this time of year. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nConfidence is increasing for soaking rainfall across the area\nfor Thursday and Friday as a low pressure system is expected to\ntrack north up the east coast for Thursday. The coastal low \ncomes ashore into the Carolinas. This will bring deformation\nprecip to the area for Thursday and Friday. It appears the dry\nslot for this system will be just to the east into CTP's area.\nIt is important to note that any slight shift in the track to\nthe west could put the PBZ area in the dry slot. For now, the\nheavy precip solution will be most prominent. \n\nLooking at the probabilities for this system, much of the area\nshows a 70% probability over the Ohio Counties and over the far\neastern forecast area in the higher terrain. While some\nensembles are placing the dry slot to the east and some on the\nfringe of our area, there remains a potential for downslope\nwinds over the foot hills to the east. As well as the low \ncenter tracks north, the foothills appear to be impacted. This \nputs the probability of 1 inch or greater in these areas around\n40%. At this time, during the Thursday and Friday period, any\nsnowfall will remain confined to the higher terrain. The\nmentioned downslope from the southeasterly winds will be\nassisted with a strong 40 to 50 knot LL jet. Thus, some winds in\nthe higher terrain Thursday and Friday may see some near\nadvisory wind gusts.  \n\nIn any event, Thanksgiving seems wet, with most ensembles \nshowing the highest rain rates around dinner time. Black Friday\nmay be a bit more showery (with some chance of snow\naccumulations for the high terrain), but winds could be gusty \nfor much of the day. The rest of the holiday weekend seems\ncomparatively quieter.",
